@h1 Writer cheatsheet h1@

Every writer construct on one screen — the page to keep open in a second tab.
Each block is real Wit you can copy. For the why behind any of it, follow the
links at the foot of the page.

@h2 Marks & inline h2@

@@@pre
_italic_        → <em>              *bold*            → <strong>
*_both_*        → bold + italic     (never _*both*_ — inner mark stays literal)
@code x code@   → <code>            @br br@           → <br>
@u … u@   @s … s@   @sub … sub@   @sup … sup@
@mark … mark@   @small … small@   @cite … cite@      → same-named inline tag
pre@@@

@h2 Headings & structure h2@

@@@pre
@h1 … h1@  …  @h6 … h6@                     six heading levels
@article @section @header @footer          semantic wrappers
@nav @aside @main
@div |class card| … div@                    generic block (id / class only)
@span |id x| … span@                        generic inline (id / class only)
pre@@@

@h2 Blocks h2@

@@@pre
@blockquote … blockquote@     quote / epigraph
@pre … pre@                   preformatted (monospace)
@aside … aside@               note — inline OR block
@aside(class note) … aside@   a styled callout
@hr hr@                       divider
pre@@@

@h2 Lists h2@

@@@pre
@ul  @li … li@  ul@                    bullets
@ol  @li … li@  ol@                    numbered (auto)
@dl  @dt term dt@ @dd def dd@  dl@     definitions
pre@@@

@h2 Tables h2@

@@@pre
@table |rows [[H1, H2], [a, b]]| |caption Cap|     inline rows (row 0 = header)
@table |schema [k1, k2]| |rows @records|           positional schema
@table |schema { k - Label }| |rows @records|      labelled schema
|header false|   |header 2|   |header [X, Y]|      header control
[…, ! comma-safe, multi-line cell !]               wrapped cell
pre@@@

Use the plain table node — not the frozen form — for any table backed by records.

@h2 Images & layout h2@

@@@pre
@img |src a.png| |alt …| |size small|medium|large|full|240|50%| |align center|
@figure  @img |size medium|  @figcaption Fig 1. figcaption@  figure@
@row  @col |size 260| … col@  @col … col@  row@     side-by-side (cols wrap narrow)
pre@@@

@h2 Links & media h2@

@@@pre
@a |href https://x| |target _blank| text a@
@audio |src a.mp3| |controls true| audio@
@video |src v.mp4| |controls true| video@           (media is HTML-only)
pre@@@

@h2 Reusable pieces h2@

@@@pre
#year: 1923                          single-line value
#epigraph: … !!                      value block
#chapter ||number, title||           block def with captures
@h2 Chapter ::number:: h2@
...                                  ← invocation body drops in here
chapter#
@year                                use a value by name
reference ./shared/sources.wit       pull in another file
pre@@@

@h2 Comments (never rendered) h2@

@@@pre
~ line comment to end of line          (the space after ~ is required)
~~ inline or multi-line comment ~~/     (only ~~/ closes; ~/ paths are safe)
pre@@@

@h2 Build commands h2@

@@@pre
wit build doc.wit                 styled HTML document → stdout
wit build doc.wit --fragment      bare article, for embedding
wit build doc.wit -o out.html     write HTML (extension infers format)
wit build doc.wit -o out.md       Markdown (GitHub-flavoured)
wit build doc.wit -o out.pdf      PDF (needs Chrome / Chromium)
wit build doc.wit --raw           reset-only base; you style everything
wit fmt doc.wit -w                re-indent in place
wit check doc.wit                 validate (non-zero exit on error)
pre@@@

@h2 Prose safety h2@

Punctuation, quotes, and syntactic-looking numbers are all literal prose: a
year-then-period, arithmetic shapes, a decimal, and lines that open with a
greater-than sign, a dash, an asterisk, or a number. A tilde glued to a token — a
path or an approximation — is prose too.

@aside(class note) 💡 Three characters to keep off a following letter in running
prose: a bare at-sign (it breaks emails), a hash at the start of a line (it starts
a definition), and a bare vertical bar (it is swallowed). To show any of them
literally, wrap the mention in frozen inline code. aside@

